    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5302",
    ":question/problem": "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x-2}{x^2-6x+8}.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-6x+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x = 4.$ Therefore, $x = 4$ is the only vertical asymptote."
  },
