      ":question/id": "9da44686-a271-5d67-9dd0-7fb4a1f7283c",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-39802",
      ":question/problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-2}{{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-x-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{{x}^{2}(x+7)-(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{({x}^{2}-1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)\\cancel{(x+1)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+1)}(x+7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-1)(x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x-1)(x+7)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-7$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-7$ and $x=1.$"
    },
