      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-39804",
      ":question/problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{3}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x-1)(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x-1{)}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+5{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1{)}^{2}}{(x+5{)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-5.$ Therefore, $x=-5$ is the only vertical asymptote."
    },
