      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-80691",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{x(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+2}}{x\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is the only vertical asymptote."
